    <br><br>
    <p><i>A rumbling sounds deep in your chest, a roaring that calls out from your viscera. Scathing, punching, and crawling– boom! It alights.</i></p>
    <br>
    <p><i>Garaj</i> (गरज) is the Hindi word for a roar or thunder. Here, <i>Garaj</i> is not just the Hindi word for thunder, but the cracking of all that has long been gathering.</p>
    <br>
    <p>We have witnessed the rising of voices, of people out on the streets, fighting, wanting, demanding. But the people in power have gone above and beyond to repress, suppress and inflict contempt. Why then does your thunder alight? Is it the bills passed that take away your right to be as human as you want, or the trodden bits of justice you seek to claim? Is it the voice of a student standing before a barricade, demanding an education that does not discriminate? Is it the anger of a woman who has to watch her back every time she steps outside? Or perhaps the grief of a people whose homes have been reduced to rubble, whose histories are being rewritten by those who proclaim themselves pure? Is your thunder a blaring culmination of your personal conflicts, of liberties snatched from within the comfort of your home? Could it be the first time you raise your voice against a parent, a lover or a tradition that has long called for your silence?</p>
    <br>
    <p><i>“When the times darken <br> will there be singing even then?”</i></p>
    <br>
    <p>Of injustices endured, prejudices caught on from your folks, and wounds left to fester. <i>Garaj</i> is the fall of a thousand scathing silences pressing against the walls of their confinement until something gives way. It is a spark: a spark for a revolution, a spark for a new age to begin, whether it is on the streets or on the sidewalks of your heart. It is for all those who bask under the incessant rain when the thunder rolls because they know it waters the seeds of a new beginning and hopes of the change they wish to see.</p>
    <br>
    <p>Would the thunderous roar strike fear into those who hear it, sending them cowering into corners? Or would it ignite a passion, an anticipation of all that the future might hold? When the thunder falls down into the forages, you should remember that it brings with it the nursing fall of rain. Relief that ripples into all the wounds that were left to rot. Maybe that is what <i>Garaj</i> is for you. A harbinger of something lighter and calmer, born from the very unrest that has been charging within.</p>
    <br>
    <p>This is a call for entries in prose, poetry, essays and photography that capture the pulse of revolution. Issue #13 <i>Garaj</i> invites you to write from the tumult that sears through your being.</p>
    <br>
    <p>When all that has been silenced within you finally breaks, what will your thunder sound like?</p>

    <br><br>

